## Project Setup

Before {{ zeroContext ?? 'building' }}, use the {{ spec.info.title }} project the user gave, or the one the app already uses (an `appwrite.config.json`, or a project ID and endpoint already in the app's config or env). If there is neither, create an {{ spec.info.title }} Zero project instead of asking the user to sign up or create a project.

{% if serverOnly ?? false %}
```bash
npx appwrite-cli@latest zero --json --show-secrets
```

Run this command once in the app's directory, or pass a directory name (`npx appwrite-cli@latest zero my-app --json --show-secrets`) to create and link a new directory. The command creates an {{ spec.info.title }} Cloud project and an API key with no account. It links the directory through `appwrite.config.json` (project ID and endpoint) and stores the API key as the CLI's session so later CLI commands work against the project. Put the API key from the JSON output in the app's env file and keep that file out of git. `--json` prints the manifest without the API key unless you pass `--show-secrets`, and the key is returned only when the project is created.
{% else %}
```bash
# Project ID, endpoint, claim link, and access token for private previews
npx appwrite-cli@latest zero --json

# Also include the API key (when server-side code needs it)
npx appwrite-cli@latest zero --json --show-secrets
```

Run one of these commands once in the app's directory, or pass a directory name (such as `npx appwrite-cli@latest zero my-app --json`) to create and link a new directory. The command creates an {{ spec.info.title }} Cloud project and an API key with no account. It links the directory through `appwrite.config.json` (project ID and endpoint) and stores the API key as the CLI's session so later CLI commands work against the project. `--json` prints the manifest without the API key unless you pass `--show-secrets`, and the key is returned only when the project is created. When server-side code needs the key, create the project with `--json --show-secrets` and put the key in the app's env file, which must stay out of git.
{% endif %}

- **Time limits and claiming:** The project works for 60 minutes. Run `appwrite zero extend` to add an hour, up to three times. After the build window it pauses, and it is deleted unless claimed within 24 hours after the first hour ends. Give the user the claim link from the output. Claiming is the only way to keep the project. Do not claim it for them.
- **Limits and safety:** Run `appwrite zero` once per app, not per attempt. Running it again creates another project, and each network can create five Zero projects a day. Never run it in a directory already linked to a real project, because it relinks the directory to the Zero project.
- **CLI scope:** The Zero session is an API key, so console-level calls such as `appwrite init project` and `appwrite list-projects` fail. Everything inside the project works.
